      "latency_ms": 24866.5,
      "prompt_tokens": 28,
      "completion_tokens": 1175,
      "output": "**Water** is a transparent, tasteless, odorless, and nearly colorless chemical substance that is the main constituent of Earth's hydrosphere and the fluids of all known living organisms. It is essential for all known forms of life.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is from chemical, physical, biological, and planetary perspectives:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Chemical Identity\n*   **Formula:** **H₂O** (two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom).\n*   **Molecular Structure:** The molecule is **bent (V-shaped)** at an angle of ~104.5°. Because oxygen is more electronegative than hydrogen, the molecule is **polar**—it has a partial negative charge near the oxygen and partial positive charges near the hydrogens.\n*   **Hydrogen Bonding:** This polarity causes water molecules to stick to one another via **hydrogen bonds**. This relatively weak but abundant intermolecular force is responsible for almost all of water’s unique properties.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Physical States (Phases)\nWater is one of the few substances that exists naturally on Earth in all three states:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Freezes at **0°C (32°F)** at standard pressure. Unusually, ice is **less dense** than liquid water (it floats) because hydrogen bonds form a crystalline hexagonal lattice with lots of empty space.\n*   **Liquid (Water):** Exists between 0°C and 100°C. It has high surface tension, high specific heat capacity, and is an excellent solvent.\n*   **Gas (Water Vapor/Steam):** Boils at **100°C (212°F)** at standard pressure. It is a potent greenhouse gas.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Key Anomalous Properties (Why Water is \"Weird\")\nWater behaves differently than almost any other liquid due to hydrogen bonding:\n\n| Property | Significance |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Density Anomaly** | Maximum density at **4°C**. Ice floats, insulating lakes/oceans below, allowing life to survive winter. |\n| **High Specific Heat** | Absorbs huge amounts of heat with little temperature change. **Regulates Earth’s climate** and stabilizes organism body temperatures. |\n| **High Heat of Vaporization** | Evaporation cools surfaces efficiently (sweating, transpiration in plants). |\n| **Universal Solvent** | Dissolves more substances than any other liquid (salts, sugars, gases, acids). Enables biochemical reactions and nutrient transport. |\n| **Cohesion & Adhesion** | **Cohesion** (water sticking to water) creates surface tension. **Adhesion** (water sticking to other things) drives **capillary action** (water moving up plant roots/stems). |\n| **Transparency** | Allows sunlight to penetrate oceans/lakes, enabling photosynthesis by aquatic plants and phytoplankton. |\n\n---\n\n### 4. Biological Role\n*   **The Medium of Life:** All metabolic reactions occur in aqueous solution.\n*   **Transport:** Blood (mostly water) transports oxygen, nutrients, hormones, and waste.\n*   **Structure:** Provides turgor pressure in plant cells (keeps them rigid) and shape to animal cells.\n*   **Reactant:** Essential for **hydrolysis** (breaking down polymers like proteins, carbs, fats) and **photosynthesis** ($6CO_2 + 6H_2O \\rightarrow C_6H_{12}O_6 + 6O_2$).\n*   **Temperature Buffer:** High heat capacity prevents rapid temperature fluctuations in cells.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Planetary Distribution (The Hydrosphere)\n*   **~71% of Earth's surface** is covered by water.\n*   **97.5% is Saline** (oceans).\n*   **2.5% is Freshwater.**\n    *   ~68.7% locked in ice caps/glaciers.\n    *   ~30.1% groundwater.\n    *   ~0.3% surface water (lakes, rivers, swamps) + atmospheric water + biological water.\n*   **The Water Cycle (Hydrologic Cycle):** Continuous movement via evaporation, transpiration, condensation, precipitation, and runoff. Driven by solar energy.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Human Use & Challenges\n*   **Agriculture:** ~70% of global freshwater withdrawals (irrigation).\n*   **Industry:** ~19% (cooling, processing, manufacturing).\n*   **Domestic:** ~11% (drinking, sanitation, hygiene).\n*   **Energy:** Hydroelectric power; cooling for thermal/nuclear plants.\n*   **Crises:** **Water scarcity** (physical vs. economic), **pollution** (industrial, agricultural runoff, plastics, PFAS), and **climate change** altering precipitation patterns and melting glaciers.\n\n---\n\n### Summary Definition\n> **Water (H₂O) is a small, polar molecule whose hydrogen bonding creates a liquid with anomalous thermal, density, and solvent properties. These properties make it the universal biological solvent, the primary regulator of planetary climate, and the single most critical resource for human civilization.**"
